insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 1, 'Ridge minimises sum (y - b0 - z''b)^2 + lambda x sum b_j^2. Which terms does the penalty charge?', '["Every coefficient the fit prints, the intercept b0 included, at one price per unit of size.", "The squared residuals of the training rows, each times lambda.", "Only the feature coefficients b_j; the intercept b0 is left out of the penalty.", "Just the coefficients of the four well-level attributes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s basis states the objective with "the intercept b0 not penalised", so lambda multiplies the sum of the squared feature coefficients alone. The residuals sit in the first term with no lambda, and the penalty makes no exception by feature: the attributes shrink hardest because of what they fit, while every feature pays the same price.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 2, 'On the 180 training rows with the seven features, what is the ridge intercept in standardised space at lambda 100?', '["105.883333 us/ft, the training mean of DT", "1.187122 us/ft, the intercept rebuilt in original units", "105.214444 us/ft, the test-well mean", "-5453.266676 us/ft, the least squares intercept in original units"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With every feature centred on its training mean and the intercept outside the penalty, the standardised intercept is the training mean of DT, 105.883333, at every lambda. 1.187122 is the original-unit intercept at lambda 100, -5453.266676 the original-unit intercept at lambda 0, and 105.214444 is the test-well mean of DT, which the fit never sees.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 3, 'What are the effective degrees of freedom of the seven-feature ridge fit at lambda 1000?', '["7.000000", "3.458214", "0.265317", "0.965695"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ridge path prints 0.965695 at lambda 1000. 7.000000 is the count at lambda 0, where every feature is spent in full; 3.458214 belongs to lambda 100; and 0.265317 is the training R-squared about the training mean at lambda 1000, a different column.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 4, 'Of the six lambdas tried on the teaching split with the seven features, which gives the lowest test RMSE on EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8?', '["Lambda 1000, at 8.607538 us/ft.", "Lambda 100, reading 5.759287 us/ft.", "Least squares, lambda 0, at 16.999672 us/ft on those three wells.", "Lambda 10, at 9.418023 us/ft."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The test RMSE falls from 16.999672 at lambda 0 through 9.418023 at lambda 10 to 5.759287 at lambda 100, then rises to 8.607538 at lambda 1000. Lambda 100 is the lowest of the six on these three wells, and lambda 0 is the highest.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 5, 'As lambda rises from 0 to 1000 on the teaching split, what does the training R-squared about the training mean do?', '["Climbs with the penalty, from 0.265317 at lambda 0 up to 0.861914.", "It falls at every step, from 0.861914 to 0.265317.", "First falls and then rises again, tracing the same shape as the test RMSE.", "Holds at 0.861914 at every lambda."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each step in lambda pulls the fit further from the training rows, so the training R-squared about the training mean reads 0.861914, 0.861901, 0.860878, 0.833394, 0.688743 and 0.265317: a fall at every step. The falling-then-rising shape belongs to the test RMSE, and the unpenalised intercept does not stop the feature coefficients shrinking.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 6, 'The engine puts lambda on the sum of squares and does not divide it by the number of rows. What follows when the same lambda is applied to fewer training rows?', '["The penalty is unchanged, because the engine rescales lambda by the row count before it fits.", "A weaker penalty results, since fewer rows leave the fit fewer coefficients to charge for size.", "It acts as a stronger penalty: the residual sum grows with rows and the penalty term does not.", "Nothing moves at all, because every feature is standardised on the rows passed to the fit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lambda equals scikit-learn Ridge''s alpha on the same standardised features and is not divided by n. The residual term gains a square with every row while the penalty term stays the same size, so on fewer rows the same lambda weighs more. The number of coefficients does not depend on the rows, and standardising sets the spread of the features without touching the balance between the two terms.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 7, 'At lambda 10 the standardised GR coefficient is 6.235322 us/ft per training population standard deviation. What does the engine report for GR in original units?', '["0.278671 us/ft per gAPI", "6.235322 us/ft per gAPI, the same figure again", "0.317732 us/ft per gAPI, as the ridge path prints it", "0.177531 us/ft per gAPI in the original-unit table"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The original-unit coefficient is the standardised one over the feature''s training scale: at lambda 10 GR reads 0.278671 us/ft per gAPI. Standardising changes the unit, so 6.235322 is per training population standard deviation of GR and never per gAPI. 0.317732 is GR in original units at lambda 0 and 0.177531 at lambda 100.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 8, 'To convert a standardised ridge coefficient for GR back to us/ft per gAPI, which figure does the engine divide by?', '["22.437616 gAPI, the sample SD (n - 1) of GR on those same training rows", "21.363430 gAPI, the population SD of GR over every one of the 270 sonic rows", "59.844500 gAPI, the training centre of GR", "22.375203 gAPI, the population SD of GR on the 180 training rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis gives the conversion as b_j / sd_j, and ridge standardises with the population standard deviation (dividing by n) of the rows passed, here the 180 training rows: 22.375203 gAPI. The sample SD, 22.437616, divides by n - 1; 21.363430 was fitted on all 270 rows, test wells included; and 59.844500 is the centre, which is subtracted and never divided by.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 9, 'How closely do ridge at lambda 0 and `ols` agree when both are fitted on the same training rows?', '["They differ widely, because ridge standardises its features first and least squares does not.", "Only in standardised units; in original units the intercepts differ.", "To a largest relative difference of 3.44e-16 in original units, which is rounding.", "No comparison is possible: the engine refuses lambda 0."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With lambda 0 the penalty term vanishes and ridge is least squares. The engine''s ridge coefficients in original units agree with `ols` to a largest relative difference of 3.44e-16, the intercept included. Standardising moves the coefficients into other units and leaves the fitted plane where it was, and lambda must be zero or more, so 0 is accepted.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 10, 'Which standardised coefficient of the seven-feature fit changes sign between lambda 0 and lambda 100?', '["Kb, which moves from -5.095543 to -1.782262 over the same lambdas.", "Northing, from 4.173548 to -0.274348.", "Easting, which falls from 8.680036 all the way down to 0.098385.", "RHOB, which reads 2.794047 at lambda 0 and 2.453848 at lambda 100."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Northing is positive at lambda 0 and negative at lambda 100. Kb shrinks toward zero and stays negative, easting shrinks to 0.098385 and stays positive, and RHOB stays positive at both lambdas. GR, NPHI and mudWeight keep their signs too.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 11, 'How does the engine compute the effective degrees of freedom of a ridge fit?', '["By counting every coefficient printed, the intercept among them, less one for each unit of lambda.", "As the number of standardised coefficients whose absolute size is still larger than lambda.", "From n - p on the training rows, the residual count that least squares uses for s.", "As sum d_i^2 / (d_i^2 + lambda) over the singular values of the standardised features."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine sums d_i^2 / (d_i^2 + lambda) over the singular values d_i of the standardised features, so each term lies between 0 and 1 and the total is 7.000000 at lambda 0 with seven features. The intercept is not counted because it is not penalised, the count is never a comparison of coefficients with lambda, and n - p is the residual degrees of freedom of least squares.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 12, 'A caller passes a negative lambda to `ridge`. What does the engine return?', '["The engine refuses by name: \"lambda must be a finite number, zero or more\".", "A fit with the penalty''s sign reversed, rewarding large coefficients.", "The least squares fit, reading a negative lambda as 0.", "A fit at the absolute value of lambda, with a warning."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses by name, and the message is the engine''s own words: "lambda must be a finite number, zero or more". It fits nothing in place of the call, swaps in no default and attaches no warning to a result, because a refusal returns no result.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 13, 'Why does lambda 0 score the highest test RMSE on the teaching split, 16.999672 us/ft?', '["Least squares cannot use the four attributes, so it is left to predict from the three logs alone.", "Its intercept is penalised toward zero, which drags every prediction toward a DT of zero us/ft.", "Its attribute coefficients chase the six training wells'' offsets and then extrapolate to three new wells.", "The test wells hold more rows than the training wells, and a larger test set inflates the error."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At lambda 0 the seven coefficients are free to fit the six training wells'' sonic offsets through the four well-level attributes, which is variance; on three wells never seen those coefficients extrapolate. Least squares uses all seven features, the intercept is not penalised at any lambda, and the test wells hold 90 rows against 180 in training.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 14, 'With the strongest penalty tried, the ridge fit spends less than one effective coefficient. What does it still print?', '["One coefficient; the rest drop out below a count of one.", "All seven feature coefficients, each shrunk toward zero.", "Only the intercept, at the training mean of DT.", "The three log coefficients alone, the four attributes having been removed first."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ridge shrinks coefficients and removes no feature: seven are printed at every lambda, and the effective count says how much of the seven the fit is really using. The intercept is printed beside them, and the attributes stay in the fit.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade', 15, 'The teaching split points to lambda 100 for the seven features. Why is that a weak basis for choosing lambda?', '["It is a fact about three test wells drawn by one seed; another seed may favour another lambda.", "Lambda 100 is refused on other splits, because the penalty''s limits depend on the seed.", "The 5.759287 us/ft read at lambda 100 was scored on the training rows of the six wells.", "A lambda can only be chosen from the training R-squared about the training mean."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One split is one draw of wells: EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8 at seed 5. Choosing lambda from their scores tunes the model to those three wells, which is why the next module scores every well once. The seed does not limit lambda, 5.759287 is a test RMSE on the three held-out wells, and the training R-squared falls at every step whatever lambda would serve a new well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 1, '`groupKFold` with k = 3 and seed 5 shuffles the nine sonic wells to EKENE-8, EKENE-4, EKENE-5, EKENE-2, EKENE-1, EKENE-3, EKENE-10, EKENE-9, EKENE-7. Which fold tests EKENE-2?', '["Fold 3, since it is the fourth well dealt in the shuffled order.", "Fold 0: EKENE-2 sits at shuffled position 3, and 3 mod 3 is 0.", "Fold 2, because EKENE-2 is the third name in the sorted order.", "The fold that also tests EKENE-4, its neighbour in the shuffled order."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine deals the well at shuffled position q to fold q mod k, counting positions from 0. EKENE-2 is at position 3, so it joins EKENE-8 (position 0) and EKENE-10 (position 6) in fold 0. With k = 3 there is no fold 3, the deal reads the shuffled order and never the sorted one, and EKENE-4 at position 1 goes to fold 1.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 2, 'At k 3 and seed 5, which wells does the engine''s fold 1 test?', '["EKENE-10, EKENE-2 and EKENE-8", "EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7", "EKENE-3, EKENE-4 and EKENE-5, the middle of the sorted list", "EKENE-1, EKENE-4 and EKENE-9"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine numbers folds from 0, and fold 1 takes shuffled positions 1, 4 and 7: EKENE-4, EKENE-1 and EKENE-9. EKENE-10, EKENE-2 and EKENE-8 are fold 0, EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7 are fold 2, and the middle three names of the sorted list make up none of the three folds at this seed.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 3, 'For GR, RHOB and NPHI, which lambda gives the lowest mean test RMSE over the three folds at k 3, seed 5?', '["Lambda 10, at 5.826789 us/ft.", "Lambda 0, at 5.888677 us/ft.", "Lambda 100, at 6.467800 us/ft.", "Lambda 1, at 5.872012 us/ft."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The six means for the logs are 5.888677, 5.886755, 5.872012, 5.826789, 6.467800 and 9.044393 at lambdas 0, 0.1, 1, 10, 100 and 1000. The lowest is 5.826789 at lambda 10; lambda 1 and lambda 0 read a little higher and lambda 100 higher again.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 4, 'With the four well-level attributes added to the logs, what is the lowest three-fold mean the grid reaches, and at which lambda?', '["5.759287 us/ft at lambda 100", "7.214483 us/ft at lambda 10", "6.773053 us/ft at lambda 100", "8.982593 us/ft at lambda 1000, where the penalty is at its strongest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The attribute means over the three folds run 7.648016, 7.641799, 7.588505, 7.214483, 6.773053 and 8.982593, so the lowest is 6.773053 at lambda 100. 5.759287 is the attribute set''s score on the three test wells of the single teaching split, 7.214483 is lambda 10, and 8.982593 at lambda 1000 is the highest of the six.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 5, 'At lambda 1000 the attribute set reads 8.982593 us/ft against 9.044393 for the logs alone. What does that comparison say?', '["The attributes carry real information once they are penalised hard enough.", "Lambda 1000 is the attribute set''s best setting over the three folds.", "Little about the attributes: a penalty that strong pulls every prediction toward the training mean.", "That the logs overfit at lambda 1000, which the four attributes then correct."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At lambda 1000 every coefficient has been shrunk toward zero and every prediction toward the training mean, so both sets read worse than the logs at any lower lambda. The attribute set''s best mean is 6.773053 at lambda 100, and every attribute mean in the grid is above the logs'' 5.826789 at lambda 10.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 6, 'Run `groupKFold` with k = 4 on the nine sonic wells. How are the wells dealt?', '["Into folds of 3, 2, 2 and 2 wells, which is 90, 60, 60 and 60 rows.", "It is refused, since nine wells do not divide into four equal folds.", "By rows, as scikit-learn''s GroupKFold deals them, with no seed.", "2, 2, 2 and 3 wells, the extra well going to the last fold."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Round robin sends shuffled positions 0, 4 and 8 to fold 0 and two wells to each other fold, so the folds hold 3, 2, 2 and 2 wells and fold sizes differ by at most one well. k only has to lie between 2 and the number of wells, the engine balances wells and takes a seed, and the extra well lands in fold 0.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 7, 'What does `groupKFold` do with k = 9 and with k = 10 on the nine sonic wells?', '["Both are refused, since k must be smaller than the number of wells.", "Both are dealt, and at k 10 one of the ten folds is left empty.", "k 9 is refused as a degenerate count, and k 10 wraps round to one.", "k 9 is dealt as leave one well out; k 10 is refused, naming `k`."]'::jsonb, 3, 'k runs from 2 to the number of distinct wells, and k equal to that number is allowed: it is leave one well out. Ask for one more and the engine refuses in its own words: "k must be a whole number from 2 to 9 (the number of distinct groups)". It never deals an empty fold or wraps a count round.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 8, 'Under leave one well out, which setting for GR, RHOB and NPHI has the lower mean test RMSE?', '["Lambda 10, at 5.501614 us/ft, the same order as under three folds.", "Lambda 0, reading 5.495600 us/ft against 5.501614 at lambda 10.", "Neither, since the two means are equal to six decimals.", "The attribute set at lambda 100, which reads 6.288507 us/ft."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over the nine single-well folds the logs read 5.495600 at lambda 0 and 5.501614 at lambda 10, so lambda 0 is lower, the reverse of the three-fold order. The two means are close and unequal, and the attribute set at lambda 100 reads higher than either, at 6.288507.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 9, 'Why does leave one well out give the same folds whatever the seed, while k 3 does not?', '["With one well per fold, every order of the shuffle deals the same nine folds.", "The engine skips the shuffle whenever k is an odd number of folds.", "Leave one well out deals rows, and rows have no order to shuffle.", "Its seed is fixed at 5 inside the engine, so every run matches."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At k 9 each fold holds exactly one well, so the shuffle changes only which fold number a well gets and never which wells share a fold. At k 3 the seed decides which wells share a fold. Both 3 and 9 are odd, folds are always whole wells, and the seed is the caller''s.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 10, 'How does the engine''s fold rule differ from scikit-learn''s GroupKFold?', '["Both balance rows per fold; only the engine sorts the names before it deals them.", "The engine splits large wells to balance rows, while GroupKFold keeps each well whole.", "The engine balances wells and takes a seed; GroupKFold balances rows and takes none.", "GroupKFold shuffles with a seed, while the engine deals wells in plain sorted order."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Round robin over the seeded shuffle balances the number of wells, and fold sizes differ by at most one well; scikit-learn''s GroupKFold balances rows and takes no seed. Neither splits a well. On the Ekene wells every well has 30 rows, so both would balance rows here, and on a field of uneven wells they can deal different folds.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 11, 'Of the nine single-well folds fitted by least squares on the logs, which well scores the highest test RMSE?', '["EKENE-10, at 7.459642 us/ft.", "EKENE-9, at 8.291318 us/ft.", "EKENE-2, at 7.260092 us/ft.", "EKENE-1, at 3.514700 us/ft, the lowest fold of all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The nine least squares fold scores run from 3.514700 on EKENE-1 to 8.291318 on EKENE-9. EKENE-10 at 7.459642 and EKENE-2 at 7.260092 are the next two, and EKENE-1 is the lowest of the nine, so it cannot be the highest.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 12, 'On each of the nine single-well folds, how far apart are the test RMSEs of ridge at lambda 0 and least squares?', '["Up to 3.44e-16 us/ft, the gap printed for the coefficients.", "Ridge reads higher on every fold, because its penalty never quite vanishes.", "They cannot be compared, since ridge is only run with k = 3.", "At most 2.04e-14 us/ft, which is rounding."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ridge at lambda 0 is least squares, and on every fold the two test RMSEs differ by at most 2.04e-14 us/ft. 3.44e-16 is the largest relative difference between the two sets of coefficients on the teaching split, a different comparison. The penalty is exactly zero at lambda 0, and the nine folds were run for both models.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 13, 'The logs by least squares read 6.779400, 5.779067 and 5.107563 us/ft on the three folds. Which single figure do you quote for a new well?', '["The mean, 5.888677 us/ft, quoted as k-fold by wells, k 3, seed 5.", "The best fold, 5.107563 us/ft, since it shows what the model can do.", "The teaching split''s 4.282693 us/ft on EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8.", "The worst fold, 6.779400 us/ft, as a cautious upper figure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The arithmetic mean of the three fold scores, 5.888677, estimates the error on a new well like the nine, and it carries its k and seed. A single fold is one set of three wells, and 4.282693 is one draw of three wells; neither averages over every well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 14, 'Twelve settings were compared on the same three folds. What lets a reader judge how far the winning mean may flatter the choice?', '["Searching more settings until one clears the runner-up by a wide margin.", "Deciding the grid before looking at the scores, and printing all of it.", "Reporting the winning mean alone, so the runner-up does not distract.", "Refitting the winner on its best fold and quoting that fold''s score."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A grid searched until something scores well has used the folds to choose. Even a grid fixed in advance crowns the lowest of twelve means on one set of folds: for the logs, lambda 10 leads lambda 0 by 5.826789 against 5.888677, and leave one well out puts the two in the other order. Fixing the grid first and printing every mean, with k and the seed, lets a reader see that margin. Quoting the best fold or hiding the runner-up hides it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-k-fold-by-wells', 15, 'Why must every fold of a cross-validation on the Ekene wells be a set of whole wells?', '["The engine cannot fit a model on fewer than 30 rows, the size of one Ekene well.", "Whole wells make every fold hold the same number of rows on any field at all.", "Rows of one well share its sonic offset, so a split well would be scored on an offset already seen.", "`groupKFold` refuses with a message whenever a well would fall across two folds."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each Ekene well carries its own sonic offset, so its rows are not independent test cases, and a fold that split a well would test on rows whose offset the model had fitted. Row counts balance only when wells are the same size, the engine fits far smaller sets, and `groupKFold` deals whole wells by construction, so there is nothing for it to refuse.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 1, '`leakageDemo` returns `optimism` for an RMSE comparison. How does the engine compute it?', '["Random-row test RMSE less group test RMSE, positive whenever the well split reads the better of the two scores.", "As the ratio of the group test RMSE to the random-row one, which sits above 1 when the model has been flattered.", "By taking the training RMSE of the group split less its test RMSE, the gap between the seen and unseen wells.", "Group test RMSE less random-row test RMSE, so a positive figure means the random split flattered the model."]'::jsonb, 3, 'For RMSE, where lower is better, the engine''s optimism is the group test RMSE less the random-row test RMSE: positive when the random-row score looks better than the whole-well score. The reversed subtraction flips every sign, the engine returns a difference in us/ft and never a ratio, and the training score plays no part.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 2, 'At seed 5, with the logs and the four attributes, what does `leakageDemo` report?', '["Random-row 6.030242 against group 4.282693, both read at seed 5.", "Random-row test RMSE 5.311723 against group test RMSE 16.999672.", "A random-row 4.299599 and a group 11.365950, from the same table.", "Random-row 5.201187 against group 5.902813, both in us/ft of slowness."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With the attributes at seed 5 the random-row split reads 5.311723 us/ft and the group split 16.999672, an optimism of 11.687949. 6.030242 and 4.282693 are the logs alone at seed 5, 4.299599 and 11.365950 are the attributes at seed 12, and 5.201187 and 5.902813 are the attributes at seed 1.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 3, 'On the logs alone at seed 5 the optimism is -1.747549 us/ft. What does the sign say?', '["The random split flattered the model, as it must whenever rows of one well fall on both sides.", "For the logs the engine subtracts in the other order, so the sign has to be read reversed.", "The random-row split read worse than the well split on this draw: 6.030242 against 4.282693.", "The group split leaked, because EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8 shared rows with training."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative optimism means the group test RMSE, 4.282693, sits below the random-row one, 6.030242. The engine uses one subtraction for every feature set, and the group split holds out whole wells with no well on both sides. Shared rows flatter only when a feature gives the model a path to a well''s offset, and the logs give none.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 4, 'Over seeds 1 to 12, on how many seeds is the optimism positive with the attributes, and on how many is it negative on the logs alone?', '["Positive on 12 of 12 with the attributes; negative on 7 of 12 on the logs alone.", "Positive on all 12 with the attributes and on all 12 for the logs, since rows are shared every time.", "Positive on 7 of 12 with the attributes, and negative on 12 of 12 for the logs alone.", "Positive on 5 with the attributes, the same count of positives the logs show."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With the attributes the optimism is positive on 12 of the 12 seeds; on the logs alone it is negative on 7 and positive on the other 5. Rows of every well are shared on every random split, yet the logs still read worse under it on 7 seeds, because no log names a well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 5, 'Why does a random-row split flatter the model once the four well-level attributes are features?', '["Constant down each well, the attributes name it, so the model learns a well''s offset from its training rows and meets it in test.", "The attributes are measured more precisely than the logs, so they fit every row of every well better than the logs do.", "The random-row split holds out 81 rows against 90 for the group split, and a smaller test set always scores lower.", "Varying sample by sample down each well, the attributes add detail about the rock that the three logs miss."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Easting, northing, kb and mudWeight are constant down each well, so together they identify it. Under a random-row split every well has rows on both sides, and the model learns each well''s sonic offset through the attributes and meets it again in the test rows. Precision and test size play no part, and the attributes do not vary down a well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 6, 'On the logs alone, why is there no leak even though rows of all nine wells fall on both sides at seed 5?', '["The random-row split drops shared wells before fitting whenever the features are logs.", "The logs carry no well offset, because the offset is planted only in the attributes.", "Least squares cannot overfit three features, so every split scores it the same.", "No log names the well a row came from, so the model has no path to a well''s offset."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every Ekene well carries its own sonic offset in DT, and GR, RHOB and NPHI do not say which well a row belongs to, so sharing wells gives the model nothing to use. The split keeps every shared well, the offset is in the target and the attributes are unrelated to it, and the two splits do score differently, by a sign that follows the draw of test wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 7, 'At test fraction 0.3 the random-row split holds out 81 rows and the group split 90. Why do the two differ?', '["The group split rounds its test size up to whole wells, while the random-row split rounds its size down.", "The engine''s ceil(testFraction x count) is applied to 270 rows in one case and to 9 wells in the other.", "Rows from wells that fall on both sides are removed from the random-row test set before scoring.", "A group split holds out a third of the wells, whatever test fraction the caller passes to it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both splits size the test set as ceil(testFraction x count): 0.3 of 270 rows is 81 rows, and 0.3 of 9 wells rounds up to 3 wells, 90 rows. Both use the ceiling, the random-row split keeps its shared wells, and the group split''s size follows the fraction passed.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 8, 'On the logs alone the random split flattered nothing at seed 5. Does that make a random-row split safe for a log-only model on a real field?', '["Yes. Once the optimism is negative on most seeds, a random-row score can be reported for a new well.", "Yes, provided at least 7 of 12 seeds read negative, as the Ekene logs do, which settles it.", "No. A log can carry a calibration or mud effect constant down one well, and one clean result cannot show there is none.", "Only for least squares, because ridge re-standardises its features and so it can never leak."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A random split that happens to flatter nothing only failed to find a path this time: on a real field a log can carry a tool calibration, a borehole effect or a mud system constant down a well. The honest estimate for a new well holds out whole wells, whatever the count of negative seeds. Ridge re-standardising protects it from an outside scaler and gives no protection from a feature that names a well.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 9, 'Across seeds 1 to 12 with the attributes, which seed shows the largest optimism?', '["Seed 2, at 11.095034 us/ft.", "Seed 8, at 10.636928 us/ft.", "Seed 1, at 0.701626 us/ft.", "Seed 5, at 11.687949 us/ft."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The twelve optimisms with the attributes run from 0.701626 at seed 1 to 11.687949 at seed 5. Seed 2 at 11.095034 and seed 8 at 10.636928 come next, and seed 1 is the smallest of the twelve.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 10, 'On the logs alone, which seed gives the most negative optimism?', '["-1.838526 us/ft, from seed 8", "-1.747549 us/ft, from seed 5", "-1.511070 us/ft, from seed 7", "-0.047942 us/ft, from seed 1"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Of the 7 negative optimisms on the logs alone, seed 8 reads -1.838526, below seed 5''s -1.747549 and seed 7''s -1.511070. Seed 1''s -0.047942 is the negative figure closest to zero.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 11, 'Logistic regression with l2 = 1 on standardised pay features scores a test log loss of 0.126480 with a scaler fitted on the training wells and 0.126152 with one fitted on all ten. What do you conclude?', '["The all-well scaler is the better choice, because it gives the lower of the two test log losses.", "Nothing leaked at all, since a scaler never sees the pay label and is fitted on the logs themselves.", "The procedure is the defect: the test wells shaped the transform, however small the change of -3.28e-4.", "The fit is unstable under any rescaling, so logistic regression should be avoided."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The difference, all wells less training wells, is -3.28e-4 and happens to favour the leaked version. The test wells still shaped the RT centre and scale the penalised coefficient was fitted through, so the test rows reached the model before it was scored. A scaler leaks through the features it transforms, with or without the label, and a small change says nothing about instability.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 12, 'Why can a scaler fitted on every row not move a least squares prediction?', '["Least squares refuses standardised features and fits the raw logs instead.", "With an intercept, rescaling a feature changes its coefficient and leaves the fitted plane where it was.", "The all-row and training-row scalers are identical on the Ekene sonic logs, so there is nothing left to move.", "Least squares clips every scaled value outside [0, 1] before it fits."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Least squares with an intercept gives the same fitted plane under any rescaling of a feature, so predictions agree to rounding. The two scalers do differ, GR centre 59.844500 on the training rows against 60.250741 on all rows, and least squares accepts scaled features and clips nothing. A penalty is where scaling starts to matter.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 13, 'In the scaling case on the pay split, what RT centre and scale does the scaler fitted on the training wells give?', '["Centre 14.045286, scale 16.599700", "Centre 13.691967, scale 16.255862, from every one of the ten wells", "Centre 14.045286 with a scale of 16.255862, one figure from each fit", "Centre 13.691967 with a scale of 16.599700, mixing the two fits"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fitted on the training wells only, RT has centre 14.045286 and scale 16.599700; fitted on all ten wells it reads 13.691967 and 16.255862. The other two options pair a figure from one fit with a figure from the other.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 14, 'Inside cross-validation by wells, where must a preparation step such as a scaler be fitted?', '["Once, on every well before the folds are dealt, so that each fold shares one transform.", "On each fold''s test wells, since those are the rows the model will be scored on.", "In each fold, on that fold''s training wells, then applied unchanged to its test wells.", "Only for ridge; least squares takes its scaler from every row without harm."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every preparation step, scalers, fills, feature choices and lambda itself, is fitted on the training rows only, which inside cross-validation means refitted inside each fold. A transform fitted before the folds are dealt has seen every fold''s test wells, and one fitted on the test wells is the leak itself.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-leakage', 15, 'Adding the four attributes to the logs moves both split scores. On how many of the 12 seeds does it lower the random-row test RMSE, and on how many does it raise the group test RMSE?', '["Lowers the first on 7 and raises the second on 5.", "It lowers the first on 12 and raises the second on 12.", "Lowering the first on 12, it raises the second on 7 of them.", "Only 5 seeds lower the first, while all 12 raise the second."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over seeds 1 to 12 the attributes lower the random-row test RMSE on 12 and raise the group test RMSE on 12: they help on rows of wells the model has seen and hurt on wells it has not, every time. 7 and 5 are the logs'' own counts of negative and positive optimism.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 1, 'The Ekene pay rule labels a sample PAY = 1 when which condition holds?', '["PHIC is at least 0.16 and RT is at least 10 ohm.m.", "PHIC is above 0.16 or RT is above 10 ohm.m.", "The logistic probability of pay for the row is above 0.5.", "RHOB, NPHI and RT all sit on the pay side of their own cutoffs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is stated: PAY = 1 when PHIC is at least 0.16 and RT is at least 10 ohm.m, otherwise 0, and 94 of the 300 samples are pay. Both halves must hold, both inequalities include the cutoff, the label comes from the rule before any model is fitted, and RHOB and NPHI have no cutoffs in it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 2, 'A sample reads PHIC exactly 0.16 and RT exactly 10 ohm.m. What is its PAY label?', '["0, as both values must sit strictly above.", "0, since a boundary goes to class 0 as the threshold at one half does.", "1, because both halves of the rule say at least.", "Undefined; the engine refuses such a row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At least includes the cutoff, so a PHIC of exactly 0.16 and an RT of exactly 10 ohm.m both qualify and the label is 1. A rule written with above would label different rows. The class-0 rule at exactly 0.5 belongs to `predict` and says nothing about how the label was made, and a label on a cutoff is an ordinary label.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 3, 'Which inputs does the logistic pay model see, and which input of the rule does it never see?', '["PHIC and RT, the rule''s own two inputs.", "RHOB, NPHI and RT; the rule''s PHIC is withheld from it.", "GR, RHOB and NPHI, the same three logs the sonic model is fitted on.", "Every channel, PHIC included."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule reads PHIC, a core calibrated porosity, and PHIC is not a feature: the model sees RHOB, NPHI and RT and must learn the label from them. RT enters both the rule and the features, so the resistivity half is visible to the model, while the porosity half has to be read through density and neutron. GR is a sonic-model feature and plays no part here.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 4, 'At the same fraction 0.3 and seed 5, the pay split holds out EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7 while the sonic split held out EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8. Why?', '["The pay split is run at seed 6, the seed after the one the sonic split used.", "It balances pay rows across the test wells, and that moves the draw of wells.", "EKENE-6 has no sonic, so it is held out of the pay training rows every time.", "The pay split draws from all ten wells, EKENE-6 included, so the shuffle differs."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every one of the ten wells carries a PAY label, EKENE-6 among them, so the pay split shuffles ten names where the sonic split shuffled nine, and the same fraction and seed give a different draw. The seed is 5 for both, the engine has no stratified split, and EKENE-6 is one of the seven training wells.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 5, 'How many of the pay split''s 210 training rows are pay?', '["94", "24", "70", "66"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Of the 210 training rows, 70 are pay. 94 is the pay count over all 300 samples, 24 is the pay count on the 90 test rows, and 66 is the count of non-pay test rows.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 6, 'The RT coefficient is 0.241141 log odds per ohm.m. What does one more ohm.m do to the odds of pay, holding RHOB and NPHI fixed?', '["It multiplies them by exp(0.241141), which is 1.272700.", "It adds 0.241141 to the probability of pay.", "It multiplies the probability of pay by 1.272700 on every row.", "It adds 1.272700 to the log odds of pay for each extra ohm.m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A logistic coefficient adds to the log odds, so it multiplies the odds by its exponential: exp(0.241141) = 1.272700. Odds multiply and probabilities do not, since the same step moves a row near one half a lot and a row near 0 or 1 hardly at all. The step added to the log odds is 0.241141 itself.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 7, 'The RHOB coefficient is -10.716404 log odds per g/cm3. What is it per 0.01 g/cm3?', '["-10.716404, since a log odds figure carries no unit to rescale", "-0.107164 log odds", "-1.979331, which is the coefficient over its standard error", "-0.640305, the NPHI coefficient taken over its standard error"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A coefficient carries the log odds per unit of its feature, and a whole g/cm3 is larger than the spread of density on these wells, so per 0.01 g/cm3 it is a hundredth: -0.107164. -1.979331 is RHOB''s coefficient over its standard error and -0.640305 NPHI''s, and the per-unit figure changes with the unit it is read in.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 8, 'Which coefficient of the fitted pay model sits the most standard errors from zero?', '["RHOB, at -1.979331 standard errors.", "The intercept, at 1.580826 of its standard errors.", "NPHI, whose ratio of -0.640305 is the smallest in size.", "RT, at 6.212972."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Coefficient over standard error reads 1.580826 for the intercept, -1.979331 for RHOB, -0.640305 for NPHI and 6.212972 for RT, so RT is the clearest of the four by a wide margin and NPHI the least clear.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 9, 'NPHI''s coefficient is -9.876360 log odds per v/v with a standard error of 15.424455. What do the training rows say about it?', '["They do not pin down even its sign once RHOB and RT are in the model.", "Neutron porosity lowers the odds of pay, a firm finding at -0.640305 standard errors.", "The model leans on NPHI most, as its standard error is largest.", "Its coefficient is really zero once its error is counted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At -0.640305 standard errors from zero, the NPHI coefficient''s sign is not settled by these rows. A large standard error marks a coefficient the rows pin down poorly, which is the opposite of leaning on it, and the engine prints the fitted value as it is. The standard errors also assume independent rows, and rows of one well are not.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 10, 'The pay model''s deviance falls from 267.335951 to 49.014054 when the three features are added. What are these two figures?', '["Training and test deviance, the second scored on the three test wells.", "The intercept-only deviance and the fitted model''s, each -2 x log likelihood.", "The log likelihood before and after the fit, both in natural log units.", "Sums of squared residuals of the 0 and 1 labels, before and after the features."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deviance is -2 times the log likelihood: 49.014054 for the fit with RHOB, NPHI and RT, whose log likelihood is -24.507027, and 267.335951 for the intercept-only model (`nullDeviance`). Both are on the 210 training rows, and a logistic fit maximises the log likelihood, so it reports no sum of squared residuals.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 11, 'Row 61 of EKENE-3 reads RT 27.700000 ohm.m and gets a probability of pay of 0.817561, and its PAY is 0. What happened?', '["The engine mislabelled the row, because an RT above 10 ohm.m makes every sample pay by the rule.", "It is a training row, since the model was fitted on EKENE-3, so its probability says nothing.", "The rule''s porosity half failed there, and the model sees porosity only through density and neutron.", "The row was clipped to class 0 because its resistivity lies outside the range the model saw."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The label needs PHIC at least 0.16 as well as RT at least 10 ohm.m, and PHIC is not a feature, so a row with high resistivity and low core porosity can fool the model. At 0.817561 the row is called pay and is a false positive. EKENE-3 is a test well, and `predict` clips nothing.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 12, '`predict` returns a probability of exactly 0.500000 for a logistic row. Which class does it assign?', '["Class 1, because the threshold is at or above 0.5.", "It refuses a row that sits exactly on the threshold.", "Either class at random, drawn with the call''s seed.", "Class 0, since class 1 needs a probability above 0.5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis reads, in the engine''s own words, "class 1 when the probability is above 0.5, class 0 at exactly 0.5", and the engine''s own half case returns 0.500000 and class 0. At or above belongs to `rocCurve`, `predict` refuses nothing here, and it draws nothing at random.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 13, 'A training set passed to `logistic` holds one class only. What does the engine return?', '["It refuses, naming `y`: \"y must contain both classes, 0 and 1\".", "A fit whose intercept runs off toward infinity, returned with a warning.", "Coefficients of zero, with a probability of 1 on every row of that class.", "A fit on the single class, each probability its share."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Logistic regression needs both classes in the training labels, and the engine refuses by name, in its own words: "y must contain both classes, 0 and 1". A refusal returns no fit, no warning and no probabilities.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 14, 'Row 60 of EKENE-3 gets a probability of pay of 0.068312. What is the sign of its log odds eta, and why?', '["Positive, because every probability the engine returns is a positive number.", "Zero, since the row''s PAY label is 0 and eta records the label it was given.", "Negative, because p = 1 / (1 + exp(-eta)) sits below one half only when eta is below zero.", "No sign can be read, since `predict` discards eta once it has the class."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At eta = 0 the probability is exactly one half; below zero it sinks toward 0 and above zero it climbs toward 1. A probability of 0.068312 therefore has a negative eta, which is ln(p / (1 - p)). A probability is positive whatever the sign of eta, eta is computed from the features and never from the label, and the sign follows from the probability.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-logistic-regression', 15, 'RHOB and NPHI correlate at -0.563498 over the 210 training rows. What does that say about each coefficient that holds the other fixed?', '["The pair take opposite signs, one cancelling the other, since the logs move together.", "It describes a direction these rows rarely take, so its standard error grows.", "Each is exact, because correlation touches least squares and never logistic fits.", "The engine drops one of the pair once their correlation passes one half."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Holding RHOB fixed while NPHI moves asks about a direction the rows seldom take, since the two move together (-0.563498 over the training rows, -0.405407 over the 70 pay rows among them), and the standard errors show the cost. Both coefficients here are negative, so neither cancels the other; correlation affects any fit that holds a feature fixed, logistic included; and the engine drops no feature.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 1, 'In the engine''s confusion matrix for the pay test wells, what does the cell in row true 0, column predicted 1 count?', '["4 pay rows the model missed.", "62 non-pay rows called non-pay, the true negatives of pay.", "4 non-pay rows the model called pay, the false positives.", "0 pay rows called non-pay, the count of missed pay in the report."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s layout, in its own words: "matrix[i][j] counts rows whose TRUE label is labels[i] and PREDICTED label is labels[j] (scikit-learn layout)". Row true 0, column predicted 1 holds the 4 non-pay rows called pay. There are 0 false negatives, and the 62 true negatives sit on the diagonal.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 2, 'What is the precision of pay on the 90 test rows?', '["0.857143, which is 24 / (24 + 4)", "1.000000, which is 24 / (24 + 0)", "0.939394, which is 62 / (62 + 4)", "0.955556, the rows on the diagonal over all 90"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Precision of pay is TP / (TP + FP): of the 28 rows called pay, 24 are pay. 24 / 24 is the recall of pay, 62 / 66 is the recall of non-pay, and 0.955556 is the accuracy.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 3, 'What is the recall of pay on the test wells, and what does it say?', '["0.857143: of the rows called pay, that share are pay.", "0.939394, because four of the pay rows were missed.", "0.923077, the F1 of pay.", "1.000000: all 24 pay rows were called pay."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Recall of pay is TP / (TP + FN) = 24 / (24 + 0): the model found every pay row in the three test wells and paid for it with 4 false calls. 0.857143 is the precision of pay, 0.939394 is the recall of non-pay (no pay row was missed), and 0.923077 is the F1 of pay.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 4, 'Label 0, non-pay, has precision 1.000000 and recall 0.939394. Why?', '["Non-pay rows outnumber pay rows 66 to 24, and a precision rises with support.", "Every row called non-pay was non-pay, and 4 of its 66 rows were called pay.", "Four pay rows called non-pay lower its recall; its precision counts true negatives only.", "Label 0 is the negative class, so the engine holds its precision at exactly 1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read from the non-pay side, the same four counts give TP 62, FP 0 and FN 4: the 4 non-pay rows called pay are pay''s false positives and non-pay''s false negatives. So precision is 62 / 62 and recall 62 / 66. Support does not enter a precision, no pay row was called non-pay, and the engine computes label 0 like any other label.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 5, 'Which figure is the F1 of pay on the test wells?', '["0.923077, from 2 x 24 / (2 x 24 + 4 + 0)", "0.945913, the macro F1 over both of the labels", "0.956571, the F1 weighted by support", "0.968750, the F1 of the non-pay label"]'::jsonb, 0, 'F1 of pay is 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN) with TP 24, FP 4 and FN 0: 0.923077. The macro F1, 0.945913, is the plain mean of the two labels'' F1, the weighted F1, 0.956571, weights them by support, and 0.968750 is the F1 of non-pay. Three different numbers carry the same two letters in one report.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 6, 'How does the engine compute F1 for a label?', '["As the arithmetic mean of the two ratios, (precision + recall) / 2, for every label in the report.", "As TP / (TP + FP + FN), the share of rows touched by that label which the model got right.", "As 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN), equal to the harmonic mean wherever precision and recall are both defined.", "By weighting the accuracy of the report by the label''s support and dividing by the rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis reads, in the engine''s own words: "2TP / (2TP + FP + FN), the harmonic mean of precision and recall where both are defined". The count formula is also defined when one ratio is 0 / 0. The arithmetic mean does not punish imbalance, TP / (TP + FP + FN) drops the factor of two, and support enters only the weighted average.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 7, 'What are the macro and weighted precision on the pay test wells?', '["Macro 0.961905; weighted 0.928571.", "Macro 0.945913; weighted 0.956571.", "Macro 0.969697; weighted 0.955556.", "Macro 0.928571; weighted 0.961905."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The macro precision is the plain mean of 1.000000 and 0.857143, 0.928571; the weighted precision gives non-pay 66 of 90 votes and reads 0.961905. The swapped pair reverses them, 0.945913 and 0.956571 are the macro and weighted F1, and 0.969697 and 0.955556 are the macro and weighted recall.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 8, 'The weighted recall and the accuracy both read 0.955556 on the test wells. How are they related?', '["By coincidence on these three test wells, and on most other data the two would differ.", "As an identity: weighting each recall TP / support by its support leaves total TP over n, which is accuracy.", "They print alike at six decimals and part company further along, as printed figures do.", "Both are the recall of pay scaled by the share of pay rows among the 90 test rows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each label''s recall is TP over its support, so weighting by support and dividing by n cancels the supports and leaves the sum of TP over n, the accuracy. The engine computes the two and asserts a difference of 0, and the identity holds for any labels. The recall of pay here is 1.000000, which scaled by the pay share is far from 0.955556.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 9, 'A model predicts sand for four rows whose true labels are sand, shale, sand and lime. With zeroDivision 0, what is the macro precision?', '["0.833333, the mean of 0.5, 1 and 1", "0.222222, the macro F1 of the four rows", "0.166667, the mean of 0.5, 0 and 0", "0.5, the precision of sand on its own"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sand is predicted four times and right twice, a precision of 0.5; shale and lime are never predicted, so their precisions are 0 / 0 and are scored zeroDivision, 0, and listed in `undefinedRatios`. The mean of 0.5, 0 and 0 is 0.166667. With zeroDivision 1 it would be 0.833333, and 0.222222 is the macro F1.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 10, 'In the same four-row sand case, switching zeroDivision from 0 to 1 leaves the macro F1 at 0.222222. Why?', '["Shale and lime each occur once and are missed once, so FN 1 gives each an F1 of 0 by counts.", "The engine applies zeroDivision to precision and never to a recall or an F1, whatever happens.", "Macro F1 weights labels by support, and shale and lime carry too little weight to move it.", "An F1 ignores the precision of a label whenever its recall is zero, so the setting is skipped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'F1 = 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN) divides by zero only when a label never occurs and is never predicted. Shale and lime each have FN 1, so each F1 is 0 by the count formula with no convention needed, and the macro F1 is sand''s F1 and two zeros over three. zeroDivision applies wherever a denominator is 0, macro averages give each label one vote, and F1 uses the counts.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 11, 'True labels 0, 1, 1 against predicted labels 0, 1, 0. How many ratios does the engine score with zeroDivision?', '["One, the recall of label 1, since it reads only one half of its rows.", "None: every label is predicted and occurs, so no denominator is zero.", "Two, the precisions, since the setting always applies.", "Three, one for each ratio of the label that is missed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convention is used only when a denominator is 0, and here each label is predicted at least once and occurs at least once, so `undefinedRatios` is empty. A ratio that is small is still a count and is printed as it is, whatever the setting.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 12, 'A caller passes zeroDivision 0.5 to `classificationReport`. What happens?', '["Undefined ratios are scored one half, splitting the difference.", "It is rounded down to 0, the default setting, and the report runs.", "Every ratio in the report is multiplied by 0.5 before averaging.", "A refusal naming `zeroDivision`: \"zeroDivision must be 0 or 1\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'zeroDivision takes 0 or 1, and any other value is refused by name in the engine''s own words: "zeroDivision must be 0 or 1". A refusal returns no report, rounds nothing and scales nothing.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 13, 'The pay matrix''s column sums are 62 and 28. What does the 28 count?', '["The rows the model called pay, TP + FP for the pay label.", "The pay rows in the test wells, the support of label 1.", "Rows the model got wrong, its false positives and negatives.", "Pay rows of the seven training wells that the model was fitted on."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Columns are what the model called: 24 + 4 = 28 rows called pay, the denominator of the precision of pay. The row sums are the supports, 66 and 24, the model got 4 rows wrong, and the 70 training pay rows are outside the test matrix.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 14, 'Why must a report state the layout of its confusion matrix?', '["The engine transposes its matrix whenever the labels arrive as text, so the layout varies by call.", "Swapping the layout changes the count of true positives and the accuracy computed from it.", "With truth on the columns the off-diagonal cells swap, and 4 false positives read as 4 missed pay rows.", "Rows and columns carry different totals, so only one layout can sum to the 90 test rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Some tools put the prediction on the rows. The four numbers are the same, the diagonal is the same, and the accuracy is the same, but the off-diagonal cells change places, so a reader who assumes the wrong layout swaps false positives and false negatives. The engine keeps rows true and columns predicted for any labels, and both layouts sum to 90.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-the-confusion-matrix', 15, 'With 66 of the 90 test rows non-pay, what does a rule that calls every row non-pay score?', '["An accuracy of 0.955556, exactly the same figure as the fitted logistic model.", "It is right on 66 rows and finds none of the pay, which accuracy alone hides.", "A recall of pay of 1, because no pay row is ever wrongly called pay.", "Nothing at all, since the engine refuses a report with one predicted label."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Accuracy counts the diagonal, so a rule that never calls pay is right on every non-pay row, 66 of 90, while its recall of pay is 0. That is why the ratios by label are read beside accuracy. The fitted model''s accuracy, 0.955556, comes from 62 correct non-pay calls and 24 correct pay calls, and the engine reports a label that is never predicted with its precision scored zeroDivision.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 1, 'On the 90 pay test rows, which hold 90 distinct probabilities, how many points does the engine''s ROC curve carry?', '["90, one per test row, with no extra starting point.", "91: one per distinct score, plus the start at (0, 0).", "24, one per pay row, at each place the curve steps up.", "1584, one for every pair of a pay and a non-pay row."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine places one point per distinct score and starts at (0, 0) with threshold null, so 90 distinct probabilities give 91 points. The curve moves right as well as up, so it has more points than pay rows, and the 1584 pairs are how the AUC reads as a probability, with no point for each pair.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 2, 'In the tie case, yTrue 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0 against scores 0.1, 0.4, 0.4, 0.8, 0.8, 0.2, where does the curve stand at threshold 0.4?', '["FPR 0.000000 and TPR 0.666667, where the two rows scored 0.8 take it.", "FPR 0.666667 and TPR 1.000000, once the row at 0.2 is called positive.", "At two points, one per row at 0.4.", "FPR 0.333333 and TPR 1.000000, after a diagonal step."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 0.4 a positive and a negative share the score and are called together, so the curve moves up and right at once to (0.333333, 1.000000). (0, 0.666667) is the point at 0.8 and (0.666667, 1) the point at 0.2. In the engine''s own words, "equal scores are one threshold: their rows move together, a diagonal step when the classes are mixed".'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 3, 'What AUC does the engine return for that six-row tie case, and for a case in which every score is equal?', '["0.944444, and 0.500000 when every score is equal.", "1.000000, and 0 for equal scores.", "0.944444, with equal scores refused.", "0.997475 for the tie case, and 0.500000 for equal scores."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tie case has nine (positive, negative) pairs: eight won and one tied at 0.4, which counts one half, so the area is 0.944444. Every score equal gives a single diagonal step and 0.500000, a model that cannot rank at all. Ties are counted, never refused, and 0.997475 is the pay test wells'' AUC.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 4, 'On the pay test wells, 1580 of the 1584 (pay, non-pay) pairs have the pay row scoring higher, a tie counting one half. What does that fraction equal?', '["The accuracy at one half, 0.955556.", "The recall of pay, 1.000000, as every pay row outranks.", "The trapezoid AUC, 0.997475, to within 1.11e-16.", "The precision of pay, 0.857143, read from pairs won."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The basis says the trapezoid AUC "equals the Mann-Whitney probability with ties counted one half", and on these rows the two agree to 1.11e-16, which is rounding. Accuracy and precision are read at one threshold, and a recall of 1 at one half does not mean every pay row outranks every non-pay row: four pairs are lost.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 5, 'Every probability on the pay test rows is squared before `rocCurve` is called. What happens to the AUC?', '["It falls, since every probability shrinks toward zero when squared.", "Nothing: squaring keeps the order of the scores, and AUC reads only the order.", "A rise, because the pay rows'' high scores now stand further apart.", "It is refused, since the squared values are no longer probabilities between 0 and 1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Any change that keeps the scores in the same order leaves the curve''s points in the same order and the AUC unchanged; squaring numbers between 0 and 1 keeps their order. `rocCurve` takes scores, so it has no reason to refuse them. Log loss, which reads the probabilities themselves, would change.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 6, 'A model scores every pay row just above one half and every non-pay row just below it. What do AUC and log loss make of it?', '["AUC reads 1 for the perfect ranking; log loss sees how little confidence the probabilities claim.", "Both read perfect, because every row falls on its own correct side of one half.", "AUC reads 0.500000, because every score sits so close to one half.", "Log loss reads 0, since every call at the one half threshold is right."]'::jsonb, 0, 'AUC reads only the order, and every pay row outranks every non-pay row, so it is 1. Log loss charges each row -ln of the probability put on its label, and a probability near one half costs near ln 2 per row whatever the calls. 0.500000 is the AUC of a model whose scores are all equal, and log loss is 0 only for certain, correct probabilities.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 7, 'What log loss does the engine return for a probability of one half on every row?', '["0.5, the probability given to each row", "0, since no row is confident and wrong", "0.693147, which is ln 2", "34.538776, the charge on a clipped row"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each row is charged -ln(0.5) = ln 2 whatever its label, so the mean is 0.693147, the engine''s own case. 0.5 is the probability and never the loss, 0 is the loss of a certain, correct model, and 34.538776 is the charge on a row clipped at the wrong end.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 8, '`logLoss` is given yTrue 1, 0, 1, 0 and probabilities 1, 0, 0 and 0.3. What does it return?', '["Infinity, because a pay row was given a probability of 0", "34.538776, the charge carried by the third row alone", "8.723863, with 1 row clipped, the certain and wrong one", "8.723863, with 3 rows clipped"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine clips every probability to [eps, 1 - eps], so the mean of the four charges is 8.723863 and the three probabilities of exactly 0 or 1 are all counted as clipped, the two certain and right rows among them. Without the clip the answer would be infinite, and 34.538776 is the third row''s charge alone, one of the four that are averaged.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 9, 'In that case, why is the third row, a pay row given exactly 0, charged 34.538776?', '["Every row''s charge is capped at 34.538776 by a fixed ceiling in the engine.", "It is clipped to eps = 1.00e-15, and -ln(1.00e-15) is 34.538776.", "It is clipped to 2.22e-16, the machine epsilon.", "Its charge is ln 2 times the rows clipped."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine clips p to [eps, 1 - eps] with eps = 1.00e-15, so the row is charged -ln(1.00e-15) = 34.538776; there is no ceiling on the charge beyond what the clip implies. 2.22e-16 is where scikit-learn 1.9 clips, and ln 2 is the charge of a probability of one half.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 10, 'Where does the engine''s log loss clip draw its boundary?', '["At eps itself, which is clipped: p = 1.00e-15 counts as 1 clipped row in the engine''s tally.", "Only probabilities of exactly 0 or 1 are clipped by the engine, whatever the value of eps is.", "A probability equal to eps is kept: p = 1.00e-15 clips 0 rows, and p = 1.00e-16 clips 1.", "No clip is applied at all unless the caller passes an eps of its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clip is to [eps, 1 - eps], and a probability equal to eps is inside it: 1.00e-15 clips 0 rows and 1.00e-16 clips 1. Any probability below eps is clipped, whether or not it is exactly 0, and the default eps of 1.00e-15 applies when the caller passes none.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 11, 'scikit-learn 1.9 clips at the float64 machine epsilon, 2.22e-16. What would it charge that certain-and-wrong pay row, and what follows?', '["36.043653, against the engine''s 34.538776, so a log loss is compared only with its clip stated.", "34.538776, the same charge, since both tools clip at the same eps of 1.00e-15.", "Infinity, since scikit-learn applies no clip of any kind to a probability of exactly 0 or exactly 1.", "36.043653 on every row of the case, whether that row was clipped or not."]'::jsonb, 0, '-ln(2.22e-16) is 36.043653, so a row clipped at the wrong end is charged more there than the engine''s 34.538776, and the clip is a stated choice. An unclipped row is charged the same by both tools, so the difference sits only in clipped rows, and scikit-learn does clip.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 12, '`rocCurve` is given yTrue holding 2 positives and 0 negatives. What does the engine return?', '["A curve that climbs straight up the true positive rate axis from (0, 0) and stops there, returned with an AUC of 1 and no warning attached.", "An AUC of 0.500000 with a single diagonal step, the value the engine gives any set of scores that is unable to rank its rows at all.", "A curve whose false positive rate, a 0 / 0 on every point, is scored by the zeroDivision setting and listed among the undefined ratios.", "A refusal naming `yTrue`, in the engine''s words: \"yTrue must contain both classes (found 2 positive and 0 negative), or the ROC curve is undefined\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With no negatives the false positive rate divides by zero, and the engine refuses by name, naming the counts it found. It returns no curve and no AUC, and zeroDivision belongs to `classificationReport`.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 13, 'What threshold does the engine print for the ROC curve''s first point, and why?', '["0, the lowest score any row could carry.", "Null, because JSON has no infinity.", "1, the highest probability any row can have.", "The highest test probability, where the first row is called."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first point is (0, 0), where the threshold sits above every score and nothing is called positive. scikit-learn prints that threshold as infinity; the engine prints null, because JSON has no infinity. A threshold at the highest score already calls that row positive, and it is the second point.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 14, 'On the pay test wells the AUC is 0.997475, the accuracy at one half is 0.955556 and the log loss is 0.115676. Which question does the log loss answer?', '["Whether the probabilities themselves can be taken at face value.", "How many calls are right at the one half threshold on the rows.", "How well the model ranks pay above non-pay at any threshold.", "What share of the test rows had to be clipped before scoring."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Accuracy asks how many calls were right at one threshold, AUC asks how well the model ranks at any threshold, and log loss asks whether the probabilities can be believed as probabilities. No row was clipped on these test wells, and the clip count is reported beside the loss.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-roc-auc-and-log-loss', 15, 'Row 61 of EKENE-3, a non-pay row, carries a probability of pay of 0.817561. What does log loss charge it?', '["-ln(0.817561), the charge a pay row takes", "Nothing, as only clipped rows are charged", "-ln(1 - 0.817561), since its label is 0", "34.538776, the clip''s charge on a confident miss"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A non-pay row (y = 0) is charged -ln(1 - p). -ln(p) is the charge for a pay row, every row is charged and clipping only bounds the extremes, and 34.538776 is the charge of a row clipped at eps, which 0.817561 is far from.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'Between lambda 0 and lambda 100 on the teaching split, which features does the ridge penalty pull toward zero hardest, in standardised units?', '["The three logs, whose standardised coefficients are the largest of the seven at lambda 0 and so pay the most.", "The four well-level attributes: easting drops from 8.680036 to 0.098385 while GR goes from 7.109316 to 3.972291.", "Every feature by one common fraction, because lambda charges the same price per unit of each coefficient.", "Only the intercept, which falls toward zero from the training mean of DT as lambda grows past 10."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The standardised path shows the attributes shrinking hardest: easting 8.680036 to 0.098385, northing 4.173548 to -0.274348, mudWeight 8.884278 to 1.045997, while GR keeps 3.972291. The penalty is one price per unit, and what each coefficient loses depends on what it fitted; the intercept stays at 105.883333 at every lambda.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'The standardised intercept stays at 105.883333 us/ft at every lambda, yet the original-unit intercept moves from -5453.266676 at lambda 0 to 1.187122 at lambda 100. Why?', '["It is penalised in original units, while in standardised units the intercept is held outside the penalty.", "Ridge refits the intercept on the three test wells at every lambda and reports that one in original units.", "The original-unit intercept is the standardised one scaled by the training population SD of DT at each lambda.", "It is recovered as mean y less the sum of b_j mean_j / sd_j, and the b_j shrink as lambda grows."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis states the conversion: "b_j / sd_j, intercept mean y - sum b_j mean_j / sd_j". The intercept is never penalised in either unit, and the test wells play no part in any fit; as the feature coefficients shrink, the sum they subtract from the training mean changes, and the intercept in original units moves with it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Which lambda on the teaching split spends about three and a half effective coefficients, and what test RMSE does it score on EKENE-4, EKENE-5 and EKENE-8?', '["Lambda 10: 5.568235 effective degrees of freedom, test RMSE 9.418023 us/ft.", "Lambda 1000, at 0.965695 effective degrees of freedom and a test RMSE of 8.607538 us/ft.", "Lambda 100: 3.458214 effective degrees of freedom, test RMSE 5.759287 us/ft.", "At lambda 1 the count is 6.724037 and the test RMSE 15.485689 us/ft."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ridge path prints 3.458214 effective degrees of freedom at lambda 100, where the test RMSE is 5.759287. Lambda 10 spends 5.568235 and scores 9.418023, lambda 1 spends 6.724037 and scores 15.485689, and lambda 1000 spends 0.965695 and scores 8.607538.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'At lambda 0 the ridge output lists eight terms, the intercept among them, while the count of coefficients the fit spends reads 7.000000. What explains that count?', '["The sum runs over the seven standardised features only; the unpenalised intercept is not counted.", "An eighth term reserved for lambda itself, which the engine subtracts once from the total.", "The number of training wells plus one, since the six training wells each hold one term of the fit.", "One feature dropped as redundant at lambda 0, leaving seven that the fit actually spends."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The effective degrees of freedom are sum d_i^2 / (d_i^2 + lambda) over the singular values of the standardised features, and the intercept is not counted. At lambda 0 each of the seven terms is 1. Ridge drops no feature, and lambda adds no term of its own.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'At lambda 10 the engine prints mudWeight as 4.243369 in one table and 14.874015 in another. Which unit does each carry?', '["Both are us/ft per ppg, one from the standardised fit and one from a refit on original features.", "4.243369 is log odds per ppg and 14.874015 is us/ft per ppg, from the pay and sonic models.", "4.243369 is us/ft per ppg of mud weight; 14.874015 is us/ft per training standard deviation.", "4.243369 is us/ft per training population standard deviation of mud weight; 14.874015 is us/ft per ppg."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The standardised table reads each coefficient per training population standard deviation of its feature, and the original-unit table divides by that scale to give us/ft per ppg: 4.243369 and 14.874015 at lambda 10. Both come from one fit, and mud weight is a sonic-model feature with no place in the pay model.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'As lambda goes from 100 to 1000 on the teaching split, the test RMSE rises from 5.759287 to 8.607538 us/ft. What does the rise show?', '["Variance returning, because a very strong penalty frees the attribute coefficients to chase the offsets again.", "A refusal at lambda 1000, with the engine returning the least squares fit in place of the ridge one.", "Bias: the penalty now pulls the fit so far from the training rows that the training R-squared falls to 0.265317.", "Leakage between the training and test wells, which grows with lambda on the teaching split."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Past lambda 100 the penalty costs more fit than it saves in variance: the training R-squared about the training mean falls to 0.265317 at lambda 1000 and the effective degrees of freedom to 0.965695. Variance is the lambda 0 end, lambda 1000 is fitted like any other, and a group split holds whole wells out at every lambda.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'At seed 5 with the attributes, least squares on the group split reads a test RMSE of 16.999672 us/ft; ridge at lambda 100 on the same split reads 5.759287. What did the penalty change?', '["It closed a leak between rows of one well, since ridge re-standardises on the training rows alone before fitting.", "It moved the test wells, since ridge draws a fresh split of its own from the seed it is given by the caller.", "It pulled easting, northing, kb and mud weight hardest toward zero, curbing how far those coefficients carry onto the held-out wells.", "It removed the four attributes, whose standardised coefficients all reach zero exactly by lambda 100."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under a group split no well sits on both sides; the attribute coefficients fitted to six training wells extrapolate to wells never seen, and the penalty shrinks those coefficients hardest. Ridge uses the split it is given, and at lambda 100 easting still reads 0.098385 and northing -0.274348, shrunk but present.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Dealing the seed 5 shuffle of the nine sonic wells into three groups by position mod 3, which positions land in the group numbered 2?', '["Positions 2, 5 and 8: EKENE-5, EKENE-3 and EKENE-7.", "Positions 0, 3 and 6: EKENE-8, EKENE-2 and EKENE-10, every third from the start.", "Positions 6, 7 and 8, the last three wells of the shuffled order.", "Positions 1, 4 and 7."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine deals shuffled position q to fold q mod k, so fold 2 takes positions 2, 5 and 8: EKENE-5, EKENE-3 and EKENE-7. Positions 0, 3 and 6 make fold 0 and positions 1, 4 and 7 fold 1, and the deal is round robin, so no fold is a block of the last three.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'In three-fold cross-validation by wells on the nine sonic wells, how often does each well take part?', '["It is tested in exactly one fold and trains the model in the other two.", "Three times as a test well, once per fold, so that every fold is scored on all nine wells.", "Once in training and once in test, the two uses split evenly across the three folds of the deal.", "Once as a training well and twice as a test well, since every fold scores two wells for each it fits."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each fold tests on its own wells and trains on every other well, so every well is tested exactly once and trains in the remaining two folds. A fold tests 90 rows and trains on 180.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'On the leave one well out fold that tests EKENE-1, which reads lower on the logs, least squares or ridge at lambda 10?', '["Least squares, 3.514700 against the 3.564857 ridge reads on that same well.", "Ridge at lambda 10, 3.414773 against 3.514700 for least squares.", "Least squares by a hair, 3.514700 us/ft against 3.567014 for ridge at lambda 10.", "Neither of the two: at lambda 10 ridge and least squares print the same fold score."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The EKENE-1 fold reads 3.514700 by least squares and 3.414773 by ridge at lambda 10, so ridge is lower on this well. 3.564857 and 3.567014 are the EKENE-3 fold''s scores, and the two models differ on every fold at lambda 10.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'A field''s wells hold very different numbers of rows. What does the engine''s round robin deal keep balanced across the folds?', '["The count of rows per fold, splitting a long well where it must so that every fold holds the same rows.", "The count of wells per fold, to within one well; row counts follow from whichever wells land together.", "The count of pay rows per fold, as a stratified deal does, so that every fold sees both classes alike.", "Nothing at all: the folds follow the sorted names in blocks, whatever size each well happens to be."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Round robin over the shuffled wells balances the number of wells, and fold sizes differ by at most one well; with k 4 on nine wells the folds hold 3, 2, 2 and 2. The engine splits no well and has no stratified split, and it deals the shuffled order round robin, never in blocks of sorted names.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'A caller asks `groupKFold` for k = 1 on the nine sonic wells. What does the engine return?', '["A leave one well out deal, reading k 1 as one well per fold.", "One fold holding all nine wells, so the model is trained and then tested on the same 270 sonic rows.", "A refusal naming `k`: \"k must be a whole number from 2 to 9 (the number of distinct groups)\".", "Two folds, since the engine raises k to the smallest count its rule allows and then deals the nine wells."]'::jsonb, 2, 'k must lie from 2 to the number of distinct wells, and k 1 is refused by name in the engine''s own words, the same message it gives for more folds than wells. A fold that trained and tested on the same rows would test nothing unseen, and the engine never raises a k it was given.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'The attribute set at lambda 100 reads 5.759287 us/ft on the teaching split''s three test wells and 6.773053 as the three-fold mean by wells. Which figure do you quote as the error for a new well?', '["6.773053, the mean over folds in which every well is tested once, quoted with k 3 and seed 5.", "Whichever of the two is lower, since a lower RMSE shows what the model is able to do on new wells.", "5.759287, since the single split was drawn first and the folds were only run to confirm it.", "5.759287, because the teaching split already holds out whole wells and so it cannot flatter the model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One split is one draw of three wells; the three-fold mean tests all nine wells once and is the estimate for a new well like them, carried with its k and seed. The single split does hold whole wells out, which makes it honest for those three wells and says nothing about the other six.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Why must a k-fold score by wells be quoted with its seed?', '["The seed sets how many folds the engine deals out, so without the seed a reader cannot tell what k was.", "Another seed shuffles the wells differently, grouping other wells into folds and giving other scores.", "Only the seed tells a reader which rows of each well were held out of the fit.", "The engine refuses a fold score that is quoted without the seed it was dealt with."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine sorts the names, shuffles them once with the seed and deals them round robin, so the seed decides which wells share a fold and the fold scores follow. k is passed on its own, folds are whole wells, and a refusal concerns inputs, never how a score is quoted.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Once easting, northing, kb and mud weight join the logs, by how much does the row-by-row RMSE undercut the whole-well RMSE at seed 12?', '["7.066351 us/ft", "1.256217 us/ft, as read at seed 12", "11.365950 us/ft on the group split", "4.299599 us/ft from the random-row split"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At seed 12 the attributes give a random-row test RMSE of 4.299599 and a group test RMSE of 11.365950, an optimism of 7.066351. 1.256217 is the optimism on the logs alone at the same seed.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'Under `randomRowSplit` of the 270 sonic rows at fraction 0.3 and seed 5, how many wells have rows on both sides?', '["None, because a seeded split keeps each well''s rows together on one side.", "Three, the wells a group split at the same seed would hold out.", "All nine: its `sharedGroups` lists every sonic well.", "Six, the wells the group split would train on at seed 5."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 81 random test rows come from all 9 wells and each of them also trains the model, so `sharedGroups` lists EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2, EKENE-3, EKENE-4, EKENE-5, EKENE-7, EKENE-8 and EKENE-9. The seed fixes the draw of rows and keeps no well together.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'How does the engine''s basis describe what `randomRowSplit` is for?', '["Its basis calls it the honest estimate for a new well, provided the seed is stated beside the score it gives.", "A quick split for exploring a field before any whole well has been held out, in the words of its own basis.", "The default split for scoring any model whose features are logs sampled row by row down every well of a field.", "In its own words: \"leakage demonstration only: rows of one well can fall on both sides (sharedGroups); use groupSplit or groupKFold to score a model\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis names the function''s purpose in exactly those words. The honest estimate for a new well is the engine''s description of `groupSplit`, and a model is scored with `groupSplit` or `groupKFold`.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'A scaler fitted on all rows leaves a least squares prediction unchanged. Why does it move the l2 = 1 logistic pay model?', '["The logistic fit refuses standardised features, so the two scalers feed it different raw inputs.", "The all-well scaler also sees the pay labels, which it passes to the fit as an extra feature.", "A penalty on standardised features depends on their scale, and the all-well scaler sets another scale.", "Logistic regression has no intercept, so a shifted centre moves every one of its predictions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'With an intercept, least squares gives the same fitted plane under any rescaling, but a penalty charges coefficients measured per scaled unit: RT''s scale is 16.599700 from the training wells and 16.255862 from all ten, and the RT coefficient moves from 3.129011 to 3.083215. A scaler sees features only, and the pay model carries an intercept.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Which of these steps leaks inside three-fold cross-validation by wells?', '["Refitting the scaler on each fold''s training wells and applying it unchanged to that fold''s test wells.", "Taking the scaler''s centre and scale from all 270 sonic rows, then running `groupKFold` on the scaled rows.", "Choosing k and the seed before any fold is scored, then quoting the mean with both of them stated.", "Printing every fold''s test RMSE beside the mean, so a reader can see how widely the folds disagree."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A scaler fitted before the folds are dealt has seen every fold''s test wells, so the test rows shaped the transform the model was trained through. Refitting inside each fold on its training wells is the rule, and stating k, the seed and every fold score leaks nothing.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'Seed 9 gives the three logs a gap of 1.220206 us/ft between whole-well and row-by-row scoring, in the row-by-row split''s favour. How should it be read?', '["Only the draw: on the logs the sign falls either way, positive on 5 seeds and negative on 7.", "That the logs carry the wells'' sonic offsets whenever the seed is odd.", "A defect in the engine''s subtraction, which should give a negative figure on the logs.", "A leak through the logs, which name the well a row came from once rows are shared."]'::jsonb, 0, 'No log names a well, so the model has no path to a well''s offset, and the random-row score is neither systematically better nor worse: over seeds 1 to 12 the optimism is negative on 7 and positive on the rest, as the draw of test wells falls. Seed 9 is one of the positive draws.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'At seed 5 adding the attributes moves the random-row test RMSE from 6.030242 to 5.311723 and the group test RMSE from 4.282693 to 16.999672. What does the pair of moves say?', '["The random-row split is the more reliable, since it scored both feature sets on 81 rows.", "The attributes help on rows of wells already seen and hurt on wells the model has not seen.", "The attributes improve the model on every split, and the group figure is a fitting failure of that seed.", "The attributes are noise, which leaves both splits worse off by about the same amount."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With the attributes the random-row score improves because each well''s offset is learnt from its training rows and met again in test, while the group score worsens because the test wells'' offsets are absent from training. The same pattern holds on all 12 seeds.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Three test log losses sit side by side for the pay wells: 0.126480 (l2 = 1, scaler from the training wells), 0.126152 (l2 = 1, scaler from every well) and 0.115676 (no penalty). Which pair isolates the leak?', '["0.115676 against 0.126480, the unpenalised model against the penalised one on the same test wells.", "0.115676 against 0.126152, since the leaked model should read closest to the clean one.", "None, since log loss cannot register leakage and only an RMSE comparison can do so.", "0.126480 against 0.126152: one model and one penalty, the scaler fitted on different wells."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Leakage is judged by changing one thing, the rows the scaler saw, and holding the model and penalty fixed: 0.126480 on the training-well scaler against 0.126152 on the all-well one, a difference of -3.28e-4. 0.115676 comes from a different model with no penalty, and log loss scores the leaked and clean fits like any other.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Which wells train the logistic pay model at fraction 0.3 and seed 5?', '["EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2, EKENE-3, EKENE-7 and EKENE-9, the six that train the sonic model.", "EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7, the three wells the pay split holds out.", "EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2, EKENE-4, EKENE-6, EKENE-8 and EKENE-9.", "The nine sonic wells, since EKENE-6 has no sonic and carries no pay label."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pay split draws over all ten wells, and its training wells are EKENE-1, EKENE-10, EKENE-2, EKENE-4, EKENE-6, EKENE-8 and EKENE-9, 210 rows. EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7 are its test wells, and EKENE-6 carries a PAY label though it has no sonic.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'Of the first five test rows of EKENE-3, which does the pay model call pay at the one half threshold?', '["None of them, since every one of the five probabilities sits below one half.", "Row 60, at 0.068312, the first row of the well.", "Row 63, at 0.063221.", "Row 61, at 0.817561, a row whose PAY is 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Row 61 is the only one of the five above one half, at 0.817561, and its PAY is 0: a false positive. Rows 60, 62, 63 and 64 read 0.068312, 0.026632, 0.063221 and 0.004802 and are called class 0.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'The pay model''s intercept is 23.081681 log odds. What does it describe?', '["The log odds of pay where RHOB, NPHI and RT are all zero, a point far outside the rows.", "The share of the 210 training rows that are pay, 70 of them, written as log odds per ohm.m of resistivity.", "The fitted probability of pay for a row sitting at the training mean of every one of the features.", "The log odds of pay averaged over the 210 training rows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'eta = b0 + b1 x1 + ..., so the intercept is eta where every feature is zero. No rock reads a bulk density of zero, so it sits far outside the data. It is a log odds with no per-unit reading, and it is no average or share of the training rows.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'A label column passed to `logistic` reads 0, 1, 2 in its first three rows. What does the engine return?', '["Reading 2 as pay, a fit that takes any label above 0 as class 1 and returns its coefficients as usual.", "No fit: the engine stops at the third label and names the field in its own words, \"y[2] must be 0 or 1\".", "A fit on rows 0 and 1 only, with the row that carries the stray label left out.", "Coefficients with a warning attached, saying one label was clipped into the range 0 to 1 before the fit ran."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Logistic regression takes binary labels, and the engine refuses the first label other than 0 and 1 by name: "y[2] must be 0 or 1". A refusal returns no fit; the engine reinterprets nothing, drops no row and clips no label.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'How does the engine compute the standard errors of the logistic pay coefficients?', '["As sqrt(diag((X''WX)^-1)) at the solution, with W = p(1 - p).", "As s x sqrt(diag((X''X)^-1)), s^2 = RSS / (n - p), the least squares rule.", "By the spread of each coefficient across the three folds by wells.", "From the coefficient over its z value, computed at the fitted solution."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis reads, in the engine''s own words, "sqrt(diag((X''WX)^-1)) at the solution, W = p(1 - p)": the inverse information at the solution. s x sqrt(diag((X''X)^-1)) belongs to least squares, no fold enters the fit, and coefficient over standard error is derived from the standard error and cannot define it.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'Row 61 of EKENE-3, with PAY 0, is scored 0.817561 by the pay model. Where does it sit in the confusion matrix of the test wells?', '["Among the 4 in row true 0, column predicted 1: one of pay''s false positives.", "On the diagonal, among the 62 rows counted as correct at one half.", "Outside the matrix, as a row whose probability is too uncertain to be classed.", "In the true 1, predicted 0 cell, the cell of pay rows that the model missed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Its probability is above one half, so the engine assigns class 1, and its true label is 0. Rows are true labels and columns predicted ones, so it is one of the 4 in the true 0, predicted 1 cell. The true 1, predicted 0 cell holds 0 rows, and every row is classed.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'A row is scored a probability of exactly 0.5. What class does `predict` give it, and is it called positive by `rocCurve` at a threshold of 0.5?', '["Class 0 from `predict`, and negative in `rocCurve`, since both rules need a score above one half.", "Class 0 from `predict`, yet positive in `rocCurve`, which calls a row at or above its threshold.", "Class 1 from `predict`, and positive in `rocCurve`, since both rules call at or above one half.", "Neither function will take it: a probability exactly on the threshold is refused by both of them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each rule draws its own boundary. `predict` gives class 1 only above 0.5, so exactly 0.5 is class 0; `rocCurve` calls a row positive at a score at or above the threshold. Neither refuses a score on its boundary.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'Averaging the two labels'' recalls with one vote each gives which figure on EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7?', '["0.955556, the recall of each label weighted by its support.", "0.928571, the mean of the two labels'' precisions.", "0.945913, the mean of 0.968750 and 0.923077 over both labels.", "0.969697, the unweighted mean of 0.939394 and 1.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Macro averages give each label one vote: recall 0.939394 for non-pay and 1.000000 for pay give 0.969697. 0.955556 is the weighted recall, 0.928571 the macro precision and 0.945913 the macro F1.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'In the four-row case with true labels sand, shale, sand and lime, all predicted sand, what does `undefinedRatios` list?', '["The recall of lime and of shale, two labels never found.", "The precision of lime and the precision of shale.", "The precision of sand, the only label ever predicted.", "Nothing at all, since every label occurs in yTrue."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Shale and lime are never predicted, so their precisions are 0 / 0; the engine scores each zeroDivision and lists both in `undefinedRatios`. Their recalls have supports of 1 and are 0 by the counts, and sand''s precision is 2 over 4, a defined 0.5.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'Scored by 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN), which label comes out ahead on EKENE-3, EKENE-5 and EKENE-7?', '["Both labels equally, since each F1 in a binary report equals the accuracy 0.955556.", "Pay, at the 0.945913 macro F1.", "Non-pay, at 0.968750 against 0.923077 for pay.", "Pay, whose recall of 1.000000 lifts its F1 above that of the other label."]'::jsonb, 2, 'F1 = 2TP / (2TP + FP + FN): non-pay reads 0.968750 from TP 62, FP 0 and FN 4, and pay 0.923077 from TP 24, FP 4 and FN 0. 0.945913 is the macro F1 of both labels, and neither label''s F1 is the accuracy.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'In the binary pay report, what does label 1''s false positive count become when read from label 0''s side?', '["Label 0''s false negatives, which is why non-pay''s recall reads 62 / 66.", "Label 0''s true positives, since the four rows were called correctly for label 0.", "Label 0''s true negatives, the 62 rows called non-pay correctly.", "A count of its own, since the engine tallies every label from rows of its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'In a binary report the two labels'' rows are the same four counts read from each side. The 4 non-pay rows called pay are false positives for pay and false negatives for non-pay, which is why non-pay''s recall is 62 / 66.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Which weighted average equals the accuracy as an identity, for any labels?', '["The weighted F1, which reads 0.956571 on the pay test wells beside an accuracy of 0.955556.", "The weighted precision, 0.961905, because each label''s precision already counts its correct calls.", "Every weighted average, since weighting by support always cancels whatever the labels hold.", "The weighted recall: weighting TP / support by support and dividing by n leaves the sum of TP over n."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Weighting each label''s recall TP / support by its support and dividing by n gives the sum of TP over n, which is the accuracy; the engine asserts a difference of 0. The weighted F1, 0.956571, and the weighted precision, 0.961905, divide by other denominators and carry no such identity.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'In the engine''s layout, what do the row sums of the pay confusion matrix give?', '["62 and 28, the counts of rows the model predicted as 0 and as 1.", "True positives of each label, 62 for non-pay and 24 for pay.", "The 4 wrong calls, split by label.", "The supports: 66 non-pay rows and 24 pay rows."]'::jsonb, 3, 'matrix[i][j] counts rows whose true label is labels[i] and predicted label labels[j], so a row sums every prediction for one true label: the supports, 66 and 24. The column sums, 62 and 28, are the rows predicted 0 and 1, and the diagonal holds 62 and 24.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Which ratio from the pay report is the ROC curve''s true positive rate at a threshold?', '["The recall of pay at that threshold: TP / (TP + FN).", "The accuracy of all the calls made at that threshold, taken over the 90 test rows.", "The F1 of pay at the threshold, which balances the two ratios read from the matrix.", "The precision of pay, the share of rows called pay at the threshold that are pay."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ROC curve plots the true positive rate, which is the recall of pay, against the false positive rate as the threshold is lowered. Precision divides by the rows called pay, accuracy counts both classes, and F1 mixes precision and recall.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'At the one half threshold the recall of pay is 1.000000, yet the AUC is 0.997475. How can both hold?', '["A recall of 1.000000 is always paired with an AUC below 1 by the trapezoid rule.", "The AUC is taken on the training wells and the recall on the test wells, so the two differ.", "Every pay row clears one half, but some non-pay rows outscore some pay rows: 4 of 1584 pairs are lost.", "The AUC counts clipped rows as errors, and the recall has no clip to count."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Recall reads one threshold: all 24 pay rows score above one half. AUC reads the whole ranking: over the 1584 (pay, non-pay) pairs the pay row scores higher in 1580, 0.997475. Only a non-pay row scored above one half can outrank a pay row, so the lost pairs come from the 4 non-pay rows called pay. Both are on the test wells, and no row was clipped.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'How many (pay, non-pay) pairs does the pair reading of the AUC compare on the pay test wells?', '["1580, the pairs in which the pay row scores higher.", "91, one pair for each point of the curve and its start.", "1584, every pairing of 24 pay rows with 66 non-pay rows.", "90, one pair for each test row, matched to its nearest neighbour."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every pay row is paired with every non-pay row: 1584 pairs. The pay row scores higher in 1580 of them, which gives 0.997475, and 91 is the count of curve points.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'How many rows does the engine clip when it scores the log loss of the pay test wells?', '["4, one for each false positive of pay at one half.", "0: no probability on the test wells reaches the clip.", "3, the rows at exactly 0 or 1 in the engine''s case.", "All 90, since every probability is clipped to [eps, 1 - eps]."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine reports 0 rows clipped for the test log loss of 0.115676. A row is clipped only when its probability lies outside [eps, 1 - eps]; the 3 clipped rows belong to the four-row case with probabilities of exactly 0 and 1, and a false positive at 0.817561 is charged without a clip.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'The six-row tie case has scores 0.1, 0.4, 0.4, 0.8, 0.8 and 0.2. How many points does the engine''s ROC curve carry?', '["7, one point per row plus the start at (0, 0).", "4, one for each distinct score and no extra start.", "6, one point for each row of the case.", "5: four distinct scores give four points, after the null-threshold origin."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine places one point per distinct score, starting at (0, 0) with threshold null. Four distinct scores, 0.8, 0.4, 0.2 and 0.1, plus the start give 5 points, and tied rows move together as one point.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'In the four-row log loss case with yTrue 1, 0, 1, 0, what is the fourth row, y 0 with probability 0.3, charged?', '["34.538776, the charge the clip sets for a confident miss.", "Nothing: only clipped rows are charged, and 0.3 needs no clip.", "9.99e-16, the charge on an unclipped row of the case.", "0.356675, which is -ln(1 - 0.3), as its label is 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A row with y = 0 is charged -ln(1 - p), here 0.356675. 34.538776 is the third row''s charge, a pay row given exactly 0 and clipped to 1.00e-15, and 9.99e-16 is the charge on each of the two confident right rows, which are clipped too.'),
  ('mlcore', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Two test rows share one probability exactly, one pay and one non-pay. What does the engine''s ROC curve do at that score?', '["It splits the tie by row order, stepping up for the pay row first and then across for the non-pay row.", "It refuses the curve, since a threshold holding both classes has no defined point.", "It takes one diagonal step: equal scores are one threshold and their rows move together.", "It drops the two tied rows before the curve is drawn and scores the rest."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the engine''s own words, "equal scores are one threshold: their rows move together, a diagonal step when the classes are mixed". A tied pair counts one half toward the AUC; the engine neither orders the rows nor refuses or drops them.');
