-- ============================================================================
-- D2 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Machine Learning on Well Data, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed, and the question rows from the
-- committed bank JSONs under tools/course-banks/mlcore/intermediate.
--
-- What this tier owns: VALIDATING A MODEL. Ridge, its unpenalised intercept,
-- effective degrees of freedom and the bias-variance trade; cross-validation by
-- whole wells, round robin folds and leave one well out; leakage through
-- features that name a well, and a random split that flatters nothing;
-- logistic regression on a stated pay rule, log odds and the threshold at one
-- half; the confusion matrix, precision, recall and F1, macro and weighted, and
-- a zero denominator; ROC with tied scores, AUC as a probability, and log loss
-- with its clip. Digest sections 11 to 16, and section 26.
--
-- THE TRAP THE TIER IS BUILT ON. Every validation number carries its split and
-- its seed, and a leak needs a path from a test row's well to the prediction.
-- The Professional capstone grades six Professional quantities and grades no
-- condition number, separation test, Newton trace or importance.
-- ============================================================================

values ('mlcore', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-ridge-and-the-bias-variance-trade","title":"Ridge and the Bias-Variance Trade","lesson_keys":["l01-a-penalty-on-the-coefficients","l02-standardised-and-original-units","l03-an-unpenalised-intercept","l04-effective-degrees-of-freedom","l05-bias-and-variance"]},{"key":"m02-k-fold-by-wells","title":"Cross-Validation by Wells","lesson_keys":["l01-every-well-tested-once","l02-round-robin-folds","l03-leave-one-well-out","l04-choosing-lambda-by-wells"]},{"key":"m03-leakage","title":"Leakage","lesson_keys":["l01-what-leakage-is","l02-features-that-name-a-well","l03-the-optimism-of-a-random-split","l04-when-a-random-split-flatters-nothing","l05-scaling-can-leak-too"]},{"key":"m04-logistic-regression","title":"Logistic Regression","lesson_keys":["l01-a-label-from-a-stated-rule","l02-probabilities-and-log-odds","l03-reading-the-fitted-coefficients","l04-the-threshold-at-one-half"]},{"key":"m05-the-confusion-matrix","title":"The Confusion Matrix and Its Ratios","lesson_keys":["l01-rows-true-columns-predicted","l02-precision-and-recall","l03-the-harmonic-mean-and-its-averages","l04-a-zero-denominator"]},{"key":"m06-roc-auc-and-log-loss","title":"ROC, AUC and Log Loss","lesson_keys":["l01-the-roc-curve-and-tied-scores","l02-auc-as-a-probability","l03-log-loss-and-its-clip","l04-the-capstone-brief"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
